1. expected_count parsing edge cases — probed empty, %203 (leading space),
   3%20 (trailing space), %2B3 (+3), 03, 3.0, 0x3, 3e0, Arabic-indic ٣, huge
   value 99999999999999999999, -1, duplicate params (3,2 and 2,3 order), and
   expected_count in the DELETE body instead of the query, against a live
   3-CSV fixture. Every one of them was either rejected 409 (strconv.Atoi
   syntax/overflow error or genuine mismatch) or, for "+3" — a value that is
   both syntactically valid AND numerically equal to the true count —
   correctly deleted (this is a true positive, not a bypass: actual was
   really 3). Zero files were deleted by any malformed/spoofed input; no
   panic/500 on the huge-value overflow. Full transcript with codes+body+csv
   counts in this session's tool output.
2. TOCTOU — rendered page showed CSVCount=3; (a) dropped a 4th CSV into the
   fixture then DELETE expected_count=3 -> 409 "expected 3, found 4", 4 CSVs
   untouched; (b) removed a file down to 2 then DELETE expected_count=3 ->
   409 "expected 3, found 2", 2 CSVs untouched. Guard recounts via
   CountDeletableCSVs at request time in both directions; client's stale
   number is never trusted.
3. What gets deleted — built a fixture with one.csv, TWO.CSV (uppercase),
   a symlink link-to-one.csv -> one.csv, three.csv.bak, keep.json, a subdir
   with nested.csv, and a directory that merely resembles a backup dir but
   isn't the configured one. Rendered CSVCount=3 (one.csv, TWO.CSV, the
   symlink); DELETE expected_count=3 -> 200 "Deleted 3 files"; afterward
   three.csv.bak, keep.json, subdir/nested.csv, and the non-configured
   directory all survived untouched. Separately configured BUDGET2_BACKUP_DIR
   as a subdirectory INSIDE data (backupsubdir/backedup.csv): rendered
   count excluded it (count=2), DELETE expected_count=2 deleted only the two
   top-level CSVs and left backupsubdir/backedup.csv alone. Rendered count
   and actually-deleted set agreed exactly in every configuration tried —
   no split-classification drift found.
4. Single source — grep confirms `backup.CountDeletableCSVs` is the only
   call site feeding both `data["CSVCount"]` (explorer/handlers.go:816,
   consumed by the template's `expected_count` and `hx-confirm` text) and
   the DELETE guard's `actual` (backup/handlers.go:601); `isBackupDirEntry`
   is the one shared exclusion helper used by both the counter and the
   delete loop. No second CSV-counting implementation exists in this path
   (accounts.go/csvBasenames use separate glob-based counts for unrelated
   features, not this guard or this page's CSVCount).
5. Encryption/locked path — enabled password encryption on an isolated
   fixture, restarted the server to force the locked state, and confirmed
   (a) the rendered page's Clear-All button read `expected_count=0` while
   the true on-disk CSV count was 2 (encrypted files keep their .csv name),
   and (b) DELETE /data/all with expected_count=0 AND with the true count
   (2, bypassing the UI) both returned 307 redirect to /unlock via
   cmd/server/main.go's lockCheckMiddleware — neither request reached the
   handler at all, and 0 files were removed either way. Locked state is
   blocked at the routing layer, independent of the guard.
6. uid 0 — this session ran as uid 1000 (non-root), confirmed via `id -u`.
   Could not exercise root-only paths directly. By code inspection,
   HandleDeleteAllData/CountDeletableCSVs contain no root-conditional
   branches; the only uid-0-aware code in this area is in pre-existing
